const n=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"fund-pmcares-governance","title":"PM CARES: official administration and contested disclosure","sector":"Emergency funds","status":"documented","summary":"The fund's official FAQ describes PMO administration and independent private audit. Access to its tax-exemption records has been litigated.","finding":"The Delhi High Court set aside a CIC disclosure direction on 22 January 2024, addressing the Income Tax Act route and the failure to hear PM CARES as a third party. These are disclosure restrictions; they do not establish misappropriation. The FY2024–25 statement reports ₹1,279.91 crore of in-year receipts, ₹0.88 crore of payments and ₹8,452.07 crore closing balances (rounded). Receipts include ₹324.66 crore returned by implementing agencies. These aggregates warrant tracing the returns and project commitments; they do not measure final beneficiary delivery.","limitation":"January 2026 reporting describes appeal-hearing remarks, not a newly verified final judgment. No later dispositive order was verified in this review. Public-authority status, tax-record disclosure and audit scope must remain separate questions.","sources":["fund-pm-faq","fund-pm-about","fund-pm-tax-judgment","fund-pm-appeal-report","fund-pm-account-2024-25"],"entities":["PM CARES Fund","Prime Minister's Office","Delhi High Court","Central Information Commission","Income Tax Department"],"response":"The official account says contributions are voluntary and there is no budgetary support. The FY2024–25 receipts-and-payments statement bears an auditor signature dated 7 August 2026. Two AI reviewers' transcriptions agree and both years reconcile arithmetically; the separate auditor report and notes were not in the downloaded file. Disclosure litigation is distinct from whether an audit exists.","hypotheses":[{"question":"Does limited publication prevent reconciliation of specific PM CARES expenditures with delivered relief?","prediction":"A selected sanction cannot be traced across sanction, implementing agency, procurement, payment and delivery records, even after requesting available records from each public agency.","alternative":"Records are distributed among implementing ministries or hospitals, and delivery reconciles when those sources are combined.","falsifier":"A complete independently reproducible reconciliation for the selected expenditure, including discrepancies and resolutions, weakens the proposed transparency-failure mechanism.","records":"Published annual receipts and payments; Scheme sanction letters; Implementing-agency utilization certificates; Procurement awards and delivery acceptance reports; Note 11 explaining implementing-agency refunds of ₹3,24,65,80,919 in FY2024–25; Project-level balances and commitments","nextSteps":["Select one expenditure and one financial year before collecting records","Request agency-held documents through lawful public-record procedures","Publish the reconciliation and unresolved fields without treating a missing document as missing money"]}]},{"id":"fund-pmcares-foreign","title":"PM CARES: foreign contributions under an explicit exemption","sector":"Transnational finance","status":"open-question","summary":"PM CARES officially states it is exempt from all provisions of FCRA 2010 and accepts overseas donations. That is a disclosed legal arrangement, not evidence of an illicit foreign channel.","finding":"The foreign-donor identities, acceptance screening and conflict checks needed to test policy-influence theories were not established by the public pages reviewed.","limitation":"The exemption notification and a donor-level foreign ledger were not retrieved. No named donor-to-government-favour inference is supported here.","sources":["fund-pm-faq","fund-pm-about"],"entities":["PM CARES Fund","Prime Minister's Office","Ministry of Home Affairs"],"response":"The official About page presents the foreign-contribution arrangement as consistent with PMNRF practice. Emergency fundraising is an ordinary explanation for accepting overseas support.","hypotheses":[{"question":"Are conflict-of-interest checks available for overseas donors who also seek Indian government decisions?","prediction":"For a disclosed donor with a documented regulatory interest, acceptance and recusal records omit an applicable conflict review while a specific decision deviates from comparable cases.","alternative":"Donations serve emergency relief, decision-making remains separate, and apparent proximity reflects routine diplomatic or commercial activity.","falsifier":"Contemporaneous screening, recusal, independent decision reasons and comparable non-donor outcomes contradict the proposed influence mechanism.","records":"FCRA exemption notification and conditions; Published donor disclosures and corporate annual reports; Trustee conflict policy and acceptance criteria; Dated regulatory or procurement decisions","nextSteps":["Locate the exemption instrument before interpreting its scope","Use only publicly identified institutional donors","Test a specific decision against matched non-donor cases; do not infer a money transfer from organizational association"]}]},{"id":"fund-pmcares-csr","title":"PM CARES: CSR incentives and public-sector fundraising","sector":"Public resources","status":"documented","summary":"Government records document a CSR incentive and active ministerial requests for PSU donations. The 2020 guidance treated PM CARES differently from Chief Minister relief funds while allowing State Disaster Management Authority support.","finding":"MCA's 11 April 2020 FAQ recognized PM CARES contributions as CSR, excluded the specified Chief Minister/State relief funds, and recognized State Disaster Management Authority contributions. A 31 March 2020 ministerial release sought the maximum possible PSU CSR contribution to PM CARES.","limitation":"The documented policy difference and official request do not prove coercion, diversion or party benefit. The 2020 guidance is a historical policy record, not a statement of every current CSR rule.","sources":["fund-mca-csr-faq","fund-pib-psu-request","fund-ongc-allocation","fund-pm-faq"],"entities":["PM CARES Fund","Ministry of Corporate Affairs","Ministry of Chemicals and Fertilizers","ONGC","Public Sector Undertakings"],"response":"The request was publicly justified by the pandemic and described employee contributions as voluntary. PM CARES permits PSU CSR while excluding contributions from budgetary sources; these categories should not be collapsed.","hypotheses":[{"question":"Did the policy incentive materially displace previously planned local CSR relief?","prediction":"After the March 2020 request, committed local projects at affected PSUs show larger cancellations or delays than comparable projects after accounting for pandemic shutdowns and profit changes.","alternative":"Companies used uncommitted balances, added emergency expenditure or substituted centrally coordinated relief without reducing completed local assistance.","falsifier":"Board-approved project histories show no attributable reduction in promised local delivery, or similar changes occur among matched non-donating firms.","records":"PSU board and CSR committee approvals; Annual CSR schedules before and after March 2020; Project completion and beneficiary reports; Employee consent and opt-out policies in aggregate","nextSteps":["Distinguish allocation, commitment and payment dates","Build a company-year panel with pre-pandemic baselines","Seek aggregate employee-policy records without collecting personal salary data"]}]},{"id":"fund-electoral-bonds-ruling","title":"Electoral bonds: an adjudicated transparency failure","sector":"Political finance","status":"documented","summary":"The Supreme Court invalidated the electoral-bond scheme in February 2024. Its August order reproduces the holdings on voter information and unlimited corporate contributions.","finding":"ECI's 21 March 2024 release confirms publication of SBI data pursuant to the Court's directions. The lawful next step is transaction reconciliation, with donor-recipient relationships tied to actual bond identifiers.","limitation":"The ECI release was read, but the complete underlying transaction PDFs were not downloaded or independently summed in this research pass. No new party-total or company-total calculation is claimed.","sources":["fund-sc-bonds-august","fund-eci-disclosure-release","fund-eci-data-portal"],"entities":["Supreme Court of India","Election Commission of India","State Bank of India","Bharatiya Janata Party","Electoral bond donors","Political parties"],"response":"The scheme had statutory authorization when purchases were made. Invalidating the legislation is separate from proving criminality in an individual donation.","hypotheses":[{"question":"Can each asserted donor-to-party edge be reproduced from the final SBI disclosures?","prediction":"A true transaction edge has compatible purchaser, denomination, identifier and redemption records; duplicate or incomplete joins are explicitly unresolved.","alternative":"Some published claims result from name-only joins, group-company conflation, different disclosure windows or duplicate extraction.","falsifier":"The alleged edge disappears when identifiers and original PDF rows are checked, or the relevant transaction falls outside the disclosed period.","records":"Final SBI purchase and redemption files; ECI party submissions; Corporate CIN/name-change histories; Extraction checksums and row provenance","nextSteps":["Preserve source PDFs and page/row references","Join on bond identifiers with validation rather than company name alone","Display unmatched and disputed records separately","Use the same reconciliation rules for every recipient party"]}]},{"id":"fund-bonds-quid-pro-quo","title":"Donation–contract timing: a testable allegation, not a verdict","sector":"Political finance","status":"allegation","summary":"Petitioners sought an SIT into alleged electoral-bond quid pro quo. On 2 August 2024 the Supreme Court declined Article 32 intervention and directed grievances toward ordinary legal remedies.","finding":"The order treated proximity-based allegations and assumed investigative bias as assumptions requiring evidence. It was neither a finding that every donation was corrupt nor a merits exoneration of every transaction.","limitation":"No company-specific bribery conclusion follows from this record. Individual subsequent cases require separate docket verification.","sources":["fund-sc-bonds-august"],"entities":["Common Cause","Centre for Public Interest Litigation","Supreme Court of India","Electoral bond donors","Political parties","Investigative agencies"],"response":"The Court highlighted statutory remedies for individual grievances and the distinction between constitutional review of a scheme and criminal allegations.","hypotheses":[{"question":"Do donations predict exceptional procurement benefits after controlling for firm size, sector and election timing?","prediction":"A pre-specified event study shows excess favourable decisions for recipient-party jurisdictions, accompanied by independently documented procedural departures or communications evidencing an exchange.","alternative":"Large firms donate and win contracts more often; election windows, industry cycles or pre-existing bids produce apparent timing clusters.","falsifier":"The association disappears under matched controls or placebo dates, decisions were fixed before donations, or tender records demonstrate ordinary competitive treatment.","records":"Verified donation dates and recipients; Tender publication, bid, award and variation dates; All bidders and scored evaluation criteria; Corporate financials; Contemporaneous official correspondence available lawfully","nextSteps":["Pre-register windows, control group and exclusions","Compare donor and non-donor firms across governing parties","Treat timing as a lead; require decision-process evidence before upgrading status","Publish negative results and adjustment for multiple comparisons"]}]},{"id":"fund-rss-coordination","title":"RSS–BJP coordination: a public institutional connection","sector":"Institutions","status":"documented","summary":"RSS itself publicly records coordination meetings involving multiple organizations. Its September 2023 participant list names BJP president J. P. Nadda; its August 2026 report describes a further meeting of 32 organizations.","finding":"These sources support a dated participation/coordination edge. They do not by themselves support a financial-transfer, command, procurement-benefit or covert-conspiracy edge.","limitation":"Official meeting reports provide institutional self-description, not independent accounts of every private discussion. Policy influence remains issue-specific and unquantified here.","sources":["fund-rss-pune-2023","fund-rss-visakhapatnam-2026"],"entities":["Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh","Bharatiya Janata Party","J. P. Nadda","Vishva Hindu Parishad","Akhil Bharatiya Vidyarthi Parishad","Rashtriya Sewa Bharati"],"response":"The reports describe social work, organizational exchange and national/social issues. A publicly acknowledged ideological network is an ordinary explanation for joint attendance.","hypotheses":[{"question":"Did a specific documented recommendation move through an identifiable government policy process?","prediction":"Dated recommendations, subsequent meetings and draft revisions establish a sequence, while records identify which participants had decision authority.","alternative":"Shared policy positions arise independently from common ideology or public debate, without a controlling instruction.","falsifier":"The policy pre-dates the recommendation, records show contrary advice or rejection, or alternative public proposals explain the same text.","records":"Dated public resolutions and meeting reports; Consultation submissions; Draft and final policy texts; Published ministerial meeting records","nextSteps":["Choose one policy and reconstruct its chronology","Separate ideological alignment, participation, employment and legal control","Show rejected recommendations as well as adopted ones","Require financial records for every proposed funding edge"]}]},{"id":"fund-ofbjp-fara","title":"OFBJP-USA: declared representation in US filings","sector":"Transnational finance","status":"documented","summary":"A DOJ-hosted FARA filing by Overseas Friends of BJP-USA names the BJP as its foreign principal for the six-month period ending 31 August 2024. An earlier charter filing expressly describes ideological ties and BJP guidance.","finding":"The records support a disclosed representation relationship and reported diaspora outreach. A US foreign-agent registration is a disclosure status; it does not establish espionage, illicit donations or transfer of funds to India.","limitation":"The latest substantive statement read here remains the filing of 10 October 2024. An official DOJ date-range report also lists OFBJP-USA among supplemental-statement results for 31 March 2026, but that statement and its schedules were not acquired. The complete current docket and its finances remain unverified.","sources":["fund-fara-supplement-2024","fund-fara-charter-2021","fund-fara-ofbjp-2026-index"],"entities":["Overseas Friends of BJP-USA","Bharatiya Janata Party","US Department of Justice"],"response":"The registrant describes public outreach and meetings and submits financial disclosures. Expenses incurred for activities in the principal's interest must not be mislabeled as remittances to the principal.","hypotheses":[{"question":"Do publicly reported activities reconcile with filed activity descriptions and aggregate finances?","prediction":"A named event or service lacks a corresponding disclosure where one is applicable, and the gap persists after checking amendments and the correct reporting period.","alternative":"The item is reported in another filing or period, supplied in kind, conducted independently, or falls outside the applicable disclosure duty.","falsifier":"An amendment, schedule or verified classification accounts for the event and resolves the apparent discrepancy.","records":"FARA registration 6861 docket and amendments; Exhibits A/B/C/D where applicable; Supplemental financial and activity schedules; Published institutional event records","nextSteps":["Verify the full official docket and latest reporting period","Map public organizations, not private donor addresses or contact details","Label cash transfer, in-kind service and representation as different edge types","Request an institutional response before publishing a claimed omission"]}]},{"id":"fund-rss-us-lobbying-amendments","title":"RSS-related US lobbying: amended client disclosures","sector":"Transnational finance","status":"documented","summary":"Official US lobbying records preserve an original RSS client description, a later substitution of an individual’s name, and a termination effective in September 2025. Who authorized and ultimately funded the engagement remains unresolved.","finding":"The original Q3 filing names State Street Strategies (DBA One+ Strategies) on behalf of RSS. The 23 December 2025 amended registration instead names the intermediary on behalf of Vivek Sharma. The latest indexed 29 December amendments report Squire Patton Boggs income estimates of $120,000 for Q1, $100,000 for Q2 and $110,000 for Q3, totaling $330,000 across those three distinct periods. The Q3 amendment records termination effective 30 September 2025; the subject remains introductions of RSS to US officials.","limitation":"These are self-reported, rounded lobbying-income estimates at one firm, not audited remittances from RSS, transfers to India, a finding of unlawful lobbying or evidence of Modi’s direction. Count one latest report per quarter; do not add older versions or intermediary receipts. The actual contracting instructions, source of funds, explanation for amendments and complete subsequent enforcement history were not obtained.","sources":["fund-rss-lda-original-q3","fund-rss-lda-registration-amendment","fund-rss-lda-q1-amendment","fund-rss-lda-q2-amendment","fund-rss-lda-q3-termination","fund-rss-lda-report-index","fund-rss-lobbying-denial","fund-doj-fara-faq"],"entities":["Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh","Squire Patton Boggs","State Street Strategies / One+ Strategies","Vivek Sharma"],"response":"RSS spokesperson Sunil Ambekar denied engaging a US lobbying firm, according to ThePrint. The amended filings identify an individual through the intermediary. DOJ guidance makes the LDA exemption conditional; neither registration nor its absence alone settles legality.","hypotheses":[{"question":"Do the original and amended client descriptions reconcile with contemporaneous authorization and payment records?","prediction":"If the amendment obscures the actual principal, lawful disclosure of engagement instructions, invoices or contemporaneous correspondence would identify a different authorizing principal than the latest client field, beyond a mere descriptive reference to RSS.","alternative":"An independent supporter commissioned lawful advocacy about RSS; the amendment corrects imprecise client wording without concealing a foreign principal or changing the work.","falsifier":"Contemporaneous agreement and disclosed payment records establish the amended client chain and independent decision authority, with a documented explanation for the correction.","records":"Original and amended LD-1/LD-2 reports; lawful public or voluntarily released engagement letters and invoices; institutional explanation for amendments; applicable public DOJ determinations, if any","nextSteps":["Preserve versions and distinguish effective, signature and posting dates","Ask institutions for an explanation before asserting an undisclosed principal; no outreach sent in this review","Reconcile one payment chain without double-counting contractor and subcontractor receipts","Separate public advocacy, representation, funding, control and legal findings"]}]}]`),a=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"fund-pm-faq","title":"Frequently Asked Questions | PM CARES","publisher":"PM CARES / Prime Minister's Office","date":"Undated","dateType":"undated living page; footer last-updated metadata may vary by crawl","url":"https://pmcares.gov.in/en/web/page/faq","kind":"primary-official","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Q6: PMO officials administer the trust and provide support; Q8/Q12: PSU CSR accepted, budgetary-source contributions excluded; Q19: KKC & Associates LLP auditor; Q21: exemption from all FCRA provisions stated","caution":"Official institutional self-description; does not independently verify expenditure outcomes. Capture footer displayed 11 October 2026; independent review observed 12 October 2026. Accessed 14 October 2026 is the research timestamp.","lastUpdatedObserved":"2026-10-11"},{"id":"fund-pm-about","title":"About PM CARES Fund","publisher":"PM CARES / Prime Minister's Office","date":"Undated","dateType":"undated living page; footer last-updated metadata may vary by crawl","url":"https://pmcares.gov.in/en/web/page/about_us","kind":"primary-official","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Public charitable trust registered 27 March 2020; Ex-officio government-office trustees and voluntary funding explanation; Audit-account year table through 2024-25; Foreign contribution exemption compared with PMNRF","caution":"Do not infer latest cash balances or spending totals from the year table. Capture footer displayed 11 October 2026; independent review observed 12 October 2026. Accessed 14 October 2026 is the research timestamp. Inspection detail: full-text page; FY2024–25 linked statement separately inspected as PDF image","lastUpdatedObserved":"2026-10-11"},{"id":"fund-pm-tax-judgment","title":"CPIO/Dy. Commissioner of Income Tax HQ Exemption v Girish Mittal, W.P.(C) 10193/2022","publisher":"Delhi High Court; full judgment reproduced by Indian Kanoon","date":"2024-01-22","url":"https://indiankanoon.org/doc/101889778/","kind":"primary-judgment-reproduction","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Paragraphs 20–23: CIC order set aside; statutory disclosure route and third-party hearing discussed","caution":"This case concerns tax-exemption records held by the Income Tax Department; do not equate it with every RTI/public-authority issue. Government-hosted copy located at https://www.incometaxindia.gov.in/documents/d/guest/girish-mittal-rti-judgments-pdf but retrieval timed out."},{"id":"fund-pm-appeal-report","title":"Delhi high court indicates PM CARES Fund is entitled to privacy protection under RTI Act","publisher":"The Times of India","date":"2026-01-14","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/delhi/delhi-high-court-indicates-pm-cares-fund-is-entitled-to-privacy-protection-under-rti-act/articleshow/126511583.cms","kind":"secondary-court-report","access":"search-excerpt","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Reported appeal-hearing observations concerning third-party privacy","caution":"Oral observations are not a verified final appellate holding. Later status unresolved."},{"id":"fund-mca-csr-faq","title":"MCA FAQs on eligibility of CSR expenditure related to COVID-19 activities","publisher":"Ministry of Corporate Affairs / Press Information Bureau","date":"2020-04-11","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/newsite/PrintRelease.aspx?lang=2&reg=48&relid=201194","kind":"primary-official","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Q1 recognizes PM CARES CSR contributions; Q2 excludes named CM/State relief funds; Q3 allows State Disaster Management Authority COVID contributions","caution":"Historical clarification; state-level support was not categorically excluded."},{"id":"fund-pib-psu-request","title":"Union Minister urges PSUs to release CSR funds for fighting COVID-19","publisher":"Ministry of Chemicals and Fertilizers / Press Information Bureau","date":"2020-03-31","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1609467&lang=2&reg=48","kind":"primary-official","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Minister requested maximum possible CSR contributions to PM CARES; Employee contribution request expressly described as voluntary","caution":"An official request is not proof of forced payment or reprisal."},{"id":"fund-ongc-allocation","title":"ONGC announcement of Rs 300 crore CSR allocation to PM CARES","publisher":"ONGC","date":"Undated","dateType":"date not verified in retrieved excerpt","url":"https://ongcindia.com/web/eng/detail?assetClassPK=2059013&assetEntry=2059017","kind":"primary-company","access":"search-excerpt","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Company states Rs 300 crore allocated from CSR in response to PM call","caution":"Allocation is not independently verified payment; match with annual-report schedules before quantitative analysis. Inspection detail: search-excerpt; full-page retrieval timed out"},{"id":"fund-sc-bonds-august","title":"Common Cause & Anr v Union of India, W.P.(C) 266/2024 and connected petitions","publisher":"Supreme Court of India","date":"2024-08-02","url":"https://api.sci.gov.in/supremecourt/2024/17622/17622_2024_1_30_54391_Order_02-Aug-2024.pdf","kind":"primary-judicial","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Paragraphs 2–3 reproduce February invalidation/disclosure holdings; Paragraphs 12–19 distinguish timing assumptions and ordinary remedies; Paragraphs 20–21 decline Article 32 jurisdiction and dismiss petitions","caution":"No universal finding of guilt or innocence. February judgment itself was located but timed out; rely on accurately reproduced holdings in this order. Inspection detail: full-text PDF, 10 pages"},{"id":"fund-eci-disclosure-release","title":"ECI/PN/30/2024: Public disclosure of SBI electoral-bond data","publisher":"Election Commission of India","date":"2024-03-21","url":"https://www.eci.gov.in/eci-backend/public/api/download?url=LMAhAK6sOPBp%2FNFF0iRfXbEB1EVSLT41NNLRjYNJJP1KivrUxbfqkDatmHy12e%2FzBiU51zPFZI5qMtjV1qgjFsK5bO1Fe8raUy8r%2B3VvV914AW53blFZdsAx2DqtO%2FKqCSv%2B1yJkuMeCkTzY9fhBvw%3D%3D","kind":"primary-official","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"ECI confirms publication of data supplied by SBI on 21 March 2024","caution":"Release confirms publication; transaction amounts require the underlying datasets. Inspection detail: full-text PDF, 1 page"},{"id":"fund-eci-data-portal","title":"Disclosure of Electoral Bonds","publisher":"Election Commission of India","date":"Undated","url":"https://www.eci.gov.in/disclosure-of-electoral-bonds","kind":"primary-data-portal","access":"search-excerpt","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Official acquisition location for underlying SBI disclosure files","caution":"Underlying records not extracted in this pass; no computed figures supported. Inspection detail: search-excerpt; opened page requires JavaScript"},{"id":"fund-rss-pune-2023","title":"RSS Samanvay Baithak Begins in Pune","publisher":"Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh","date":"2023-09-14","url":"https://www.rss.org/Encyc/2023/9/14/RSS-Samanvay-Baithak-Begins-in-Pune.html","kind":"primary-party-organization","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Participant list names BJP president J. P. Nadda and other organizational representatives; Agenda includes national/social issues and organizational exchange","caution":"Meeting attendance supports participation, not financial transfer or legal control."},{"id":"fund-rss-visakhapatnam-2026","title":"RSS Three day Akhil Bharatiya Samanvay baithak concludes","publisher":"Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh","date":"2026-08-21","url":"https://www.rss.org/Encyc/2026/8/21/rss-three-day-akhil-bharatiya-samanvay-baithak-concludes.html","kind":"primary-party-organization","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"RSS describes coordination gathering of 32 organizations and service/social discussion","caution":"Self-reported agenda; not an independent transcript of closed discussions."},{"id":"fund-fara-supplement-2024","title":"OFBJP-USA FARA supplemental statement, registration 6861","publisher":"US Department of Justice; submitted by OFBJP-USA","date":"2024-10-10","url":"https://efile.fara.gov/docs/6861-Supplemental-Statement-20241010-9.pdf","kind":"primary-regulatory-filing","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Period ends 31 August 2024; Item 9 identifies BJP as represented foreign principal; Item 12 describes political activity and appendix records meetings; Financial schedules distinguish receipts and activity expenditures","caution":"Registrant assertions; no inference that every receipt is a transfer to India or BJP. Current 2026 status not established by this 2024 filing. Inspection detail: full-text PDF, 17 pages"},{"id":"fund-fara-charter-2021","title":"OFBJP-USA Exhibit C, registration 6861","publisher":"US Department of Justice; submitted by OFBJP-USA","date":"2021-07-06","url":"https://efile.fara.gov/docs/6861-Exhibit-C-20210706-2.pdf","kind":"primary-regulatory-filing","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Charter describes ideological relationship and following BJP guidance; Organizational fundraising purposes are described","caution":"Charter intentions are distinct from proof of a specific transaction or unlawful act. Inspection detail: full-text PDF, 17 pages"},{"id":"fund-pm-account-2024-25","title":"PM CARES Receipts and Payments Account for year ending 31 March 2025","publisher":"PM CARES Fund; statement signed by KKC & Associates LLP","date":"2026-08-07","dateType":"auditor signature date on statement; fund signature dated 6 August 2026","url":"https://pmcares.gov.in/assets/donation/pdf/Audited_Statement_2024_25.pdf","kind":"primary-audited-statement","access":"pdf-visual","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"FY2024–25 and comparative FY2023–24 receipts/payments statement is publicly available; Header states Amount in Rs.; figures are integer rupees, not lakh; Statement references notes 1–16 and separate auditor report, neither included in this PDF; Two independent visual transcriptions agree on all 34 amount cells; arithmetic residuals are zero","caution":"One-page signed statement only, not a complete audit report or project-delivery audit. Receipts-side total includes opening balance. Dashes preserved as null. See pmcares-accounts-extract.json for exact amounts and verification. Official PDF downloaded and visually inspected locally at 300dpi; independent reviewer used a separate high-magnification rendering. Preserve canonical source-access enum in site schema.","sourcePdfSha256":"b854546de57c70e457b5a46acaa640983ab514930139a685135aa5ce86b60cfc"},{"id":"fund-rss-lda-original-q3","title":"Squire Patton Boggs: original 2025 third-quarter lobbying report","publisher":"US Congress LDA database; submitted by Squire Patton Boggs","date":"2025-10-16","url":"https://lda.gov/filings/public/filing/19aeec68-06c0-4289-aba7-bf672705eeec/print/","kind":"primary-regulatory-record","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Original client field names State Street Strategies (DBA One+ Strategies) OBO Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh; income estimate $110,000; subject concerns introducing RSS to US officials.","caution":"Original version has a later amendment. Preserve it as version history, never as the latest client designation."},{"id":"fund-rss-lda-registration-amendment","title":"Squire Patton Boggs: amended lobbying registration","publisher":"US Congress LDA database; submitted by Squire Patton Boggs","date":"2025-12-23","url":"https://lda.gov/filings/public/filing/2079e138-c523-46a5-9e1d-7ccad6150263/print/","kind":"primary-regulatory-record","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Client field names State Street Strategies (DBA One+ Strategies) OBO Vivek Sharma; effective registration date 16 January 2025; signature 23 December 2025.","caution":"Registration effective date differs from submission/signature date. Personal address omitted. Filing is a registrant assertion, not proof of ultimate funding or authorization."},{"id":"fund-rss-lda-q1-amendment","title":"Squire Patton Boggs: 2025 Q1 amended lobbying report","publisher":"US Congress LDA database; submitted by Squire Patton Boggs","date":"2025-12-29","url":"https://lda.gov/filings/public/filing/97ca1b0a-a1bc-464b-b90e-6032316aad88/print/","kind":"primary-regulatory-record","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Latest indexed Q1 amendment reports $120,000 estimated lobbying-related income; client is intermediary OBO Vivek Sharma; signed 11:10:05 AM on 29 December 2025.","caution":"Q1 classification comes from the official report index because text extraction omits checkbox states. Amount is rounded reported income, not a bank transfer or direct RSS payment."},{"id":"fund-rss-lda-q2-amendment","title":"Squire Patton Boggs: 2025 Q2 amended lobbying report","publisher":"US Congress LDA database; submitted by Squire Patton Boggs","date":"2025-12-29","url":"https://lda.gov/filings/public/filing/19029198-a324-49aa-b766-5f8e878765cf/print/","kind":"primary-regulatory-record","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Latest indexed Q2 amendment reports $100,000 estimated lobbying-related income; client is intermediary OBO Vivek Sharma; signed 11:14:57 AM on 29 December 2025.","caution":"Q2 classification comes from the official report index. Do not count original and amended reports as separate expenditure or add an intermediary income total."},{"id":"fund-rss-lda-q3-termination","title":"Squire Patton Boggs: 2025 Q3 termination amendment","publisher":"US Congress LDA database; submitted by Squire Patton Boggs","date":"2025-12-29","url":"https://lda.gov/filings/public/filing/ea207624-f182-4075-a514-17b740b4ee95/print/","kind":"primary-regulatory-record","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Reports $110,000 estimated income; client is intermediary OBO Vivek Sharma; termination date 30 September 2025; signature 29 December 2025 11:21:43 AM; lobbying subject remains RSS introductions.","caution":"Termination effective date is not filing date. Report index labels Q3 termination amendment; checkboxes are not visible in extracted text. Not a legal finding about disclosure compliance."},{"id":"fund-rss-lda-report-index","title":"Official LDA search results for client Vivek Sharma","publisher":"US Congress LDA database; submitted by Squire Patton Boggs","date":"Undated; accessed 2026-10-14","url":"https://lda.gov/filings/public/filing/search/?affiliated_organization=&affiliated_organization_country=&client=vivek+sharma&client_country=&client_ppb_country=&client_state=&foreign_entity=&foreign_entity_country=&foreign_entity_ownership_percentage_max=&foreign_entity_ownership_percentage_min=&foreign_entity_ppb_country=&house_id=&lobbyist=&lobbyist_conviction_date_range_from=&lobbyist_conviction_date_range_to=&lobbyist_conviction_disclosure=&lobbyist_covered_position=&registrant=&registrant_country=&registrant_ppb_country=&report_amount_reported_max=&report_amount_reported_min=&report_dt_posted_from=&report_dt_posted_to=&report_filing_uuid=&report_house_doc_id=&report_issue_area_description=&report_period=&report_year=&search=search","kind":"primary-regulatory-record","access":"index-only","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Ten records map original reports and later amendments, including the latest 29 December 2025 Q1/Q2 amendments and Q3 termination amendment.","caution":"Dynamic index is a retrieval snapshot, not a full enforcement docket. Historical record labels in the index may reflect current client metadata; retain original form versions. Shortened search URL failed; use this inspected full query."},{"id":"fund-rss-lobbying-denial","title":"RSS response to reported US lobbying engagement","publisher":"ThePrint","date":"2025-11-13","url":"https://theprint.in/diplomacy/disclosures-to-us-senate-claim-lobbyists-with-links-to-pakistan-also-hired-for-rss-organisation-denies/2783594/","kind":"secondary-response-reporting","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Attributes Sunil Ambekar’s denial that RSS had engaged a US lobbying firm.","caution":"Secondary reporting for the denial only; original linked X statement could not be retrieved. No independent response solicited."},{"id":"fund-doj-fara-faq","title":"DOJ FARA frequently asked questions","publisher":"US Department of Justice","date":"Undated; accessed 2026-10-14","url":"https://www.justice.gov/nsd-fara/frequently-asked-questions","kind":"primary-regulatory-record","access":"full-text","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Explains foreign-principal scope, conditional LDA exemption and that filing acceptance does not certify accuracy or compliance.","caution":"General official guidance, not an adjudication of the RSS engagement. FARA is not limited to foreign governments or political parties."},{"id":"fund-fara-ofbjp-2026-index","title":"DOJ FARA supplemental-statement date-range result: 31 March 2026","publisher":"US Department of Justice","date":"Undated; accessed 2026-10-14","url":"https://efile.fara.gov/ords/fara/f?p=1381:25:9173573334954:::25:P25_DATERANGE,P25_FROMDATE,P25_TODATE:Y,03/31/2026,03/31/2026","kind":"primary-regulatory-record","access":"index-only","accessedAt":"2026-10-14","supports":"Official report lists OFBJP-USA registration 6861 and BJP as foreign principal among supplemental-statement results for the 31 March 2026 date filter.","caution":"Index-level access only: no corresponding 2026 statement or financial schedule was acquired. Does not establish current complete docket, covered period, receipts, filing compliance or transfer."}]`),e={cases:n,sources:a},r=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"solar-contracts-us-proceedings","title":"Solar contracts: allegations and dismissed US fraud counts","sector":"Energy / international finance","status":"allegation","summary":"US prosecutors alleged in November 2024 that solar-contract bribery was concealed from investors. The relevant fraud counts against Gautam Adani, Sagar Adani and Vneet Jaain were dismissed with prejudice on 10 August 2026 at prosecutors' request.","finding":"The 2024 DOJ release describes alleged promised bribes exceeding $250 million and investor misstatements. Counts 2–4 against Gautam Adani, Sagar Adani and Vneet Jaain were dismissed with prejudice on 10 August 2026, without a merits ruling. The 3 September 2026 order declined dismissal of the remaining counts without prejudice. The original charging record distinguishes the FCPA-conspiracy charge against Ranjit Gupta, Cyril Cabanes, Saurabh Agarwal, Deepak Malhotra and Rupesh Agarwal from the obstruction-conspiracy charge against the latter four; Ranjit Gupta was not listed among those charged with obstruction. SEC release LR-26554 describes proposed consent judgments with $6 million and $12 million penalties, subject to court approval.","limitation":"No conviction is established. Adani Group denies wrongdoing. The August court accepted that the $10 billion US investment pledge did not determine dismissal. The SEC consents neither admit nor deny allegations; this review has not verified an entered final civil judgment or payment. Criminal docket developments after 3 September 2026 remain unverified. No reviewed source establishes Modi’s personal involvement.","sources":["proc-doj-solar-2024","proc-adani-order-2026","proc-reuters-adani-2026","proc-adani-september-order","proc-sec-proposed-consents"],"entities":["Gautam Adani","Sagar Adani","Vneet Jaain","Adani Green Energy","US Department of Justice","US Securities and Exchange Commission"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Can specific procurement decisions be independently linked to the alleged promised payments?","prediction":"Authenticated approval dates, contract amendments and lawfully disclosed payment evidence should corroborate one another at transaction level.","alternative":"Commercially justified contracts and ordinary project negotiations may explain timing and meetings.","falsifier":"Authenticated records showing the proposed recipient had no relevant authority, or that the alleged transfer and decision chronology cannot coincide, weaken the specific linkage.","records":"SECI tenders and award notices; state electricity-regulator orders; power-sale agreements; public US docket exhibits; corporate disclosures. Obtain current docket entries before changing legal-status labels."}]},{"id":"iran-lpg-ofac-settlement","title":"Iran-origin LPG: a documented US sanctions settlement","sector":"Oil and gas / transnational trade","status":"documented","summary":"OFAC announced a $275 million settlement with Adani Enterprises on 18 May 2026 concerning 32 apparent Iran-sanctions violations.","finding":"The signed agreement describes approximately $192.1 million in US-dollar transfers for LPG purchases and supplier-origin and vessel-tracking warning signs. This creates a specific documentary trail for an import-chain investigation.","limitation":"The settlement expressly releases relevant civil liability without a finding of fault. It is neither a criminal conviction nor a final agency determination that violations occurred, and is not an admission by the respondent. The agreement records screening, cooperation and remediation. Its factual narrative is not a court adjudication and does not establish Modi’s knowledge or direction.","sources":["proc-ofac-release","proc-ofac-agreement"],"entities":["Adani Enterprises","Adani Ports and Special Economic Zone","Office of Foreign Assets Control","Mundra Port"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Did claimed cargo origins conflict with independently observable loading capability and vessel movements?","prediction":"Shipment-specific origin declarations should diverge from port infrastructure and independently corroborated voyage evidence.","alternative":"AIS errors or lawful transshipment can explain some anomalies; a tracking gap alone establishes neither origin nor intent.","falsifier":"Authenticated export records, terminal-loading records and consistent bills of lading supporting the declared origin rebut an individual mismatch.","records":"Public settlement exhibits; lawfully accessible bills of lading; port calls; dated terminal infrastructure records; licensed vessel datasets. Do not infer undisclosed supplier identities from nationality or shared addresses."}]},{"id":"rafale-judicial-record","title":"Rafale: procurement allegations and the Supreme Court's rejection","sector":"Defence procurement / judiciary","status":"documented","summary":"The Supreme Court dismissed Rafale review petitions on 14 November 2019. Its judgment is essential counterevidence to claims that corruption was judicially established.","finding":"The Court declined intervention on procurement, pricing and offsets, reiterated the limits of Article 32 review, and found no substantial material establishing government commercial favouritism in the underlying judgment. It also corrected wording about the CAG/PAC reporting sequence.","limitation":"A limited judicial-review outcome is not a forensic audit of every payment or an assurance about later events. Conversely, allegations in petitioners' submissions are not the Court's findings. Distinguish Anil Ambani's businesses from Mukesh Ambani's Reliance Industries.","sources":["proc-rafale-supreme-court","proc-rafale-government-response"],"entities":["Ministry of Defence","Dassault Aviation","Dassault Reliance Aerospace","Supreme Court of India"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Were offset deliverables completed and valued under the actual contract terms?","prediction":"Claimed credits should reconcile with accepted deliverables, due dates and valuation rules.","alternative":"Different aircraft packages, lifecycle support and technology requirements can explain headline price differences.","falsifier":"Independent acceptance and reconciled like-for-like cost records rebut a specified overstatement.","records":"Unclassified CAG reports; parliamentary answers; offset-credit acceptance records; corporate filings; public court orders. Seek lawful disclosures and respect classified material restrictions."}]},{"id":"coal-auction-competition","title":"Coal auctions: competition concerns and a closed CCI complaint","sector":"Natural resources / competition","status":"documented","summary":"A 2025 CCI order records coal-auction collusion allegations and closes the complaint without finding a prima facie competition-law violation.","finding":"The order recounts CAG competition concerns about 2015 auctions and allegations about later auctions. CCI found insufficient evidence; independent third-party participation weakened some claims, while the 2015 challenge also faced a limitation-period problem.","limitation":"The complainant's descriptions of financial ties and bidding patterns are allegations. The ministry denied procedural lapses. CAG's original report could not be fetched in this review; its content here is mediated through the CCI order. Shared ownership or a low bid alone does not establish collusion.","sources":["proc-cci-coal-order","proc-cag-coal-catalog"],"entities":["Ministry of Coal","Competition Commission of India","CESC","Adani Enterprises","Cavill Mining","Gujarat Mineral Development Corporation"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Do bidder relationships predict reduced competition after mine characteristics are controlled?","prediction":"A pre-specified comparison would show persistent differences in independent bidders, bidding activity and revenue share.","alternative":"Geology, mine readiness, transport cost and tender eligibility explain variation.","falsifier":"Differences disappear after matching mine characteristics and ownership at the auction date.","records":"Tender rules; auction logs released lawfully; MCA ownership filings; ministry action-taken notes; mine attributes; current CCI appeal status."}]},{"id":"ujjwala-connection-versus-use","title":"Ujjwala: connections versus sustained use","sector":"Welfare / household energy","status":"documented","summary":"Historical audit reporting identified low refill use despite expanded LPG access. Government reporting in 2026 records later improvement, so the older number must not be displayed as current.","finding":"The Indian Express reported CAG's 2019 finding of 3.66 annual refills for 1.93 crore beneficiaries with over one year of enrolment as of March 2018. A June 2026 PIB note reports average use rising from 3.68 in FY2021–22 to 4.71 in FY2025–26.","limitation":"These are different cohorts and periods. Low use can reflect affordability and fuel stacking, not fabricated beneficiaries. The original CAG PDF was identified but could not be fetched; the historical statistic is explicitly secondary reporting. National averages conceal distributional differences.","sources":["proc-ujjwala-historical-reporting","proc-ujjwala-government-2026","proc-ppac-data"],"entities":["Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas","Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana","Public-sector oil marketing companies","Comptroller and Auditor General of India"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Does measuring connections overstate sustained clean-cooking use in particular districts?","prediction":"Some matched district cohorts may show high connection coverage but persistently low refill rates and continued biomass use.","alternative":"Small households, migration, electric cooking or purchasing under another household connection reduce recorded refills.","falsifier":"Representative surveys and reconciled consumption histories show sustained clean-fuel use despite low recorded refills.","records":"Aggregate cohort refill distributions; public subsidy rules; PPAC state data; representative household energy surveys; grievance-resolution statistics. Avoid collecting personal beneficiary identifiers."}]},{"id":"defence-offset-delivery","title":"Defence offsets: delivery and verification gaps","sector":"Defence procurement / industrial policy","status":"documented","summary":"The original July 2026 Public Accounts Committee report documents defence-offset delivery, claim-verification and planning weaknesses; its portfolio figures require distinct denominators and dates.","finding":"For 26 ongoing contracts, paragraph 18 reports USD 9,915.38 million of obligations and USD 5,467.82 million of submitted claims: 44.86% had not been submitted within that denominator. The separate 56-contract portfolio has obligations extending to 2033. The committee criticises unrealised projects, weak planning and verification delays. Submitted claims are not automatically accepted credits or delivered technology.","limitation":"The unsubmitted share is not a measure of overdue obligations across every contract. Contract-specific maturity schedules and accepted-delivery records remain unavailable here. Paragraphs 15 and 21 report different claimed totals at different dates; their definitions and revisions need reconciliation. These portfolio findings do not establish a Rafale kickback or coordinated misconduct by particular vendors.","sources":["proc-offsets-prs-2026","proc-offsets-pac-primary-2026"],"entities":["Public Accounts Committee","Ministry of Defence","Defence Offset Management Wing","Defence Research and Development Organisation"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Are some vendors receiving credits without delivery accepted under the agreed terms?","prediction":"An obligation-level reconciliation finds accepted credits unmatched to verifiable deliverables or premature credits before acceptance.","alternative":"Reporting lags, revised milestones and valid credit multipliers create apparent mismatches.","falsifier":"Audited acceptance records, amendments and valuation reconciliations account for the credits.","records":"The original PAC report is acquired. Still needed: contract-level maturity schedule; anonymised claim register; acceptance certificates; penalty and recovery records; published action-taken reports."},{"question":"Why do the report's later-dated claimed totals differ from its earlier snapshot?","prediction":"An obligation-level version ledger identifies the claims, scope changes or corrections accounting for the USD 214.55 million difference between paragraphs 15 and 21.","alternative":"The two snapshots use different reporting populations, definitions or corrected submissions rather than a common cumulative series.","falsifier":"Published definitions, version history and reconciled claim-level totals fully explain the difference without an unexplained residual.","records":"Underlying Ministry submissions dated 8 December 2025 and 14 January 2026; claim identifiers; additions, withdrawals and corrections; definitions and reporting populations."}]},{"id":"russian-oil-pass-through","title":"Russian-oil discounts: who captures the benefit?","sector":"Oil / pricing / public finance","status":"open-question","summary":"A testable distribution question: how much of discounted imported crude is reflected in consumer prices, taxes and refiner margins? No corruption finding follows from imports or discounts alone.","finding":"Reuters documented changing delivered Urals discounts and state refiners adjusting purchases in August 2025. PPAC provides official import, retail-price, tax and subsidy series suitable for building a reproducible comparison. No pass-through estimate has yet been calculated here.","limitation":"Historical discounts are not current quotations. Freight, insurance, crude quality, exchange rates, inventories, product yields, taxes, price stabilisation and earlier losses complicate the comparison. Aggregate trade data cannot identify secret payments or beneficial ownership.","sources":["proc-russian-oil-reuters","proc-ppac-data"],"entities":["Indian Oil Corporation","Bharat Petroleum Corporation","Hindustan Petroleum Corporation","Mangalore Refinery and Petrochemicals","Petroleum Planning and Analysis Cell"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"After full landed-cost adjustments, did identifiable savings accrue disproportionately to refiner margins?","prediction":"A lagged model finds sustained excess margins associated with discount exposure after tax and cost changes are reconciled.","alternative":"Savings offset freight, inventory losses, subsidies or stabilised retail prices during cost spikes.","falsifier":"An audited cost bridge explains the apparent residual without unexplained transfers.","records":"PPAC series; customs aggregates; audited segment accounts; refinery yields; price build-ups; parliamentary pricing answers. Publish assumptions and sensitivity ranges before conclusions."}]}]`),s=[{id:"proc-doj-solar-2024",title:"Solar-contract indictment announcement",url:"https://www.justice.gov/usao-edny/pr/billionaire-chairman-conglomerate-and-seven-other-senior-business-executives-indicted",publisher:"US Department of Justice, EDNY",date:"2024-11-20",access:"full-text",supports:"Historical allegations and presumption of innocence; not current case status.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-adani-order-2026",title:"United States v. Adani, 24-CR-0433: August 10 memorandum and order",url:"https://www.casemine.com/judgement/us/6a7dbeb1002176238713a1f4/amp",publisher:"US District Court EDNY; full opinion mirrored by CaseMine",date:"2026-08-10",access:"full-text",supports:"Counts 2–4 dismissed with prejudice; no opinion on merits; investment pledge non-consideration; other counts then reserved. Web refs turn8view3, turn16view1.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-reuters-adani-2026",title:"US judge dismisses criminal case against Indian billionaire Adani",url:"https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/us-judge-dismisses-criminal-case-against-indian-billionaire-adani-2026-08-10/",publisher:"Reuters",date:"2026-08-10",access:"full-text",supports:"August criminal dismissal and Adani Group denial. Civil resolution described by reporting; primary SEC proposed-consent status is separately recorded and final entry/payment unverified.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-ofac-release",title:"Settlement Agreement between OFAC and Adani Enterprises Limited",url:"https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20260518",publisher:"US Treasury, OFAC",date:"2026-05-18",access:"full-text",supports:"$275 million civil settlement; 32 apparent sanctions violations; regulator's description.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-ofac-agreement",title:"OFAC–Adani Enterprises signed settlement, ENF 1444722",url:"https://ofac.treasury.gov/media/935636/download?inline=",publisher:"US Treasury, OFAC",date:"2026-05-18",access:"full-text",supports:"Shipment/payment narrative, screening, warning signs, cooperation and remediation; IV.1 releases civil liability without finding of fault; IV.3 says no final agency violation determination or admission. Web refs turn20view2, turn70view2.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-rafale-supreme-court",title:"Rafale review judgment, Supreme Court of India",url:"https://api.sci.gov.in/supremecourt/2019/58/58_2019_1_1502_18275_Judgement_14-Nov-2019.pdf",publisher:"Supreme Court of India",date:"2019-11-14",access:"full-text",supports:"Review dismissal, Article 32 scope, procurement findings and corrected CAG/PAC wording.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-rafale-government-response",title:"Government response to Supreme Court Rafale review judgment",url:"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1591674&lang=2&reg=48",publisher:"Press Information Bureau, Government of India",date:"2019-11-14",access:"search-excerpt",supports:"Government's account of review dismissal; use judgment for operative legal findings.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-cci-coal-order",title:"CCI Case 02 of 2025: Deepika v. CESC and others",url:"https://www.cci.gov.in/images/antitrustorder/en/order1753956227.pdf",publisher:"Competition Commission of India",date:"2025-07-31",access:"full-text",supports:"Allegations, Ministry response, competition analysis, limitations and section 26(2) closure. Separate paragraphs recounting complaint from Commission findings.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-cag-coal-catalog",title:"Report No. 20 of 2016: Union Ministry of Coal e-Auction compliance audit",url:"https://cag.gov.in/en/audit-report/details/23038",publisher:"Comptroller and Auditor General of India",date:"2016",access:"search-excerpt",supports:"Existence and scope of original audit; original full text retrieval failed.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-ujjwala-historical-reporting",title:"Steady decline in Ujjwala refills triggers fresh concerns",url:"https://indianexpress.com/article/business/steady-decline-in-ujjwala-refills-triggers-fresh-concerns-6195399/",publisher:"The Indian Express",date:"2019-12",access:"full-text",supports:"Historical CAG refill cohort and usage statistic, as secondary reporting.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-ujjwala-government-2026",title:"Infrastructure at the Core of India's Transformation",url:"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressNoteDetails.aspx?ModuleId=3&NoteId=158838&lang=10&reg=23",publisher:"Press Information Bureau, Government of India",date:"2026-06",access:"full-text",supports:"Government-reported PMUY average refill improvement to 4.71 in FY2025–26; not an independent audit.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-offsets-prs-2026",title:"Management of Defence Offsets: committee report summary",url:"https://prsindia.org/policy/report-summaries/management-of-defence-offsets",publisher:"PRS Legislative Research",date:"2026-07",access:"full-text",supports:"Secondary summary of the July 2026 PAC report and verification issues. The separately inspected original report now supplies the denominator, schedules and claim-stage qualifications.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-offsets-pac-primary-2026",title:"Public Accounts Committee, Fiftieth Report: Management of Defence Offsets",url:"https://sansad.in/getFile/app/lsscommittee/Public%20Accounts/18_Public_Accounts_50.pdf?source=app",publisher:"Lok Sabha Secretariat / Public Accounts Committee",date:"2026-07-23",access:"full-text",supports:"Original 45-page report. Paragraphs 15, 18, 19 and 21 distinguish claim snapshots, 26 ongoing contracts and the 56-contract portfolio extending to 2033; Part II records the committee's criticisms and recommendations. Presented to Lok Sabha 23 July and laid in Rajya Sabha 22 July 2026.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-russian-oil-reuters",title:"Indian state refiners eye Russian oil as discounts widen",url:"https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/indian-state-refiners-eye-russian-oil-discounts-widen-sources-say-2025-08-14/",publisher:"Reuters",date:"2025-08-14",access:"full-text",supports:"Historical delivered-price discounts and procurement response; not evidence of corruption or current market prices.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-ppac-data",title:"Import/Export of Crude Oil and Petroleum Products",url:"https://ppac.gov.in/import-export",publisher:"Petroleum Planning & Analysis Cell, Government of India",date:"2026-10-14 (page update)",access:"full-text",supports:"Official data portal and linked import, price, tax, subsidy and PMUY series. Dynamic table values not retrieved; no numerical calculation based on this page.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-adani-september-order",title:"United States v. Adani, 24-CR-0433: September 3 memorandum and order, docket 57",url:"https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.nyed.522760/gov.uscourts.nyed.522760.57.0.pdf",publisher:"US District Court EDNY; primary order archived by CourtListener RECAP",date:"2026-09-03",access:"full-text",supports:"Prior independent evidence reviewer read the order: denial without prejudice of dismissal for the remaining counts against five nonappearing defendants; this does not mean each defendant faces every remaining count. Reaffirmation of August dismissal of counts 2–4 for the three appearing defendants. Later docket not verified; the continuation reviewer’s repeat PDF retrieval failed.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"proc-sec-proposed-consents",title:"Litigation Release 26554: SEC files proposed final judgments against Gautam Adani and Sagar Adani",url:"https://www.sec.gov/enforcement-litigation/litigation-releases/lr-26554",publisher:"US Securities and Exchange Commission",date:"2026-05-14",access:"full-text",supports:"Consents without admission or denial; proposed $6m/$12m civil penalties explicitly subject to court approval. Does not establish later entry of judgment or payment. Web refs turn70view1, turn71view0.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"}],i={cases:r,sources:s},o=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"inst-pmjay-controls","title":"PM-JAY: beneficiary and claim controls flagged by CAG","sector":"Welfare / healthcare","status":"documented","summary":"CAG's 2023 performance audit identified weaknesses in PM-JAY registration and claims systems for September 2018–March 2021.","finding":"CAG recorded 749,820 beneficiaries against one placeholder mobile number and ₹6.97 crore paid across 3,903 claims involving 3,446 patients previously marked dead. These are audit anomalies, not an adjudicated theft total. The Health Ministry replied that mobile numbers do not determine eligibility, and delayed pre-authorisation and parent–child card use can explain some mortality and overlapping-treatment records. CAG rejected the generic back-dating explanation for its flagged earlier-death cases and called for investigation.","limitation":"Responsibility spans NHA, state health agencies and hospitals. Neither the audit nor the ministry's general explanation settles every disputed claim. No evidence inspected establishes a minister-directed conspiracy. Remediation needs claim-level verification.","sources":["inst-cag-pmjay-2023","inst-mohfw-pmjay-response"],"entities":["National Health Authority","Ministry of Health and Family Welfare","State Health Agencies","Empanelled hospitals"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Did weak validation permit payment for services that were not delivered?","prediction":"A blinded sample would contain paid claims lacking contemporaneous admission, procedure and discharge evidence after legitimate delayed-entry and shared-card cases are excluded.","alternative":"Data-entry errors, emergency treatment and family-card rules generate apparently impossible records despite actual treatment.","falsifier":"Independent chart review reconciles the flagged dates, patient identities and services, with no excess unsupported claims over matched controls.","records":"De-identified claim ledger; timestamped authorisation and mortality logs; sampled hospital charts; recovery and de-empanelment orders; CAG action-taken notes."}]},{"id":"inst-pmkvy-monitoring","title":"PMKVY: reused training evidence and unreliable inspections","sector":"Welfare / skills","status":"documented","summary":"CAG Report 20 of 2025 documents weaknesses in the first three phases of the flagship skills programme.","finding":"In its RPL-BICE monitoring review, CAG found 45 inspection reports reusing photographs for different trainings and 80 instances of inspectors recorded at locations in different states on the same day. The ministry cited shared logins, upload problems and image mix-ups; it reported recovery requests for 24 batches and June 2025 changes introducing individual logins and real-time geotagged photographs. CAG found the old arrangements defeated independent monitoring.","limitation":"These findings concern tested records and identified processes, not every certificate or the entire programme expenditure. Reused evidence is a serious control failure; central political direction and personal enrichment have not been established by the inspected report.","sources":["inst-cag-pmkvy-2025"],"entities":["Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship","National Skill Development Corporation","Sector Skill Councils","Training providers"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Did payment and certification incentives reward reported volume despite unverifiable training?","prediction":"Providers with suspect evidence would receive milestone payments while independent trainee confirmation or employment verification disproportionately fails.","alternative":"Real training occurred but shared credentials, weak archives and clerical uploads corrupted the monitoring trail.","falsifier":"Independent trainee and employer checks, original metadata and reconciled payment records substantiate the disputed batches.","records":"Batch-level payment and recovery ledgers; original geotagged images; individual inspector logs; consent-based trainee callbacks; employer confirmations; post-2025 re-audit."}]},{"id":"inst-pegasus-oversight","title":"Pegasus: surveillance allegations and the accountability gap","sector":"Surveillance / accountability","status":"allegation","summary":"The Supreme Court ordered an independent technical inquiry in October 2021; the identity and legal authority of any Indian operator remain unresolved in the inspected material.","finding":"The 2021 order found the Union's limited affidavit inadequate and held that national security did not eliminate judicial review. It established an expert committee. An April 2025 hearing report describes five of 29 examined devices with suspicious malware but no committee confirmation that it was Pegasus; petitioners were still seeking disclosure. This does not establish who deployed spyware.","limitation":"The full technical report was not inspected. The observer's case page was last updated July 2025; a final 2026 docket status was not verified. A target list, infection finding, operator attribution and unlawful targeting are separate evidentiary questions.","sources":["inst-sc-pegasus-2021","inst-sco-pegasus-2025","inst-sco-pegasus-status"],"entities":["Union of India","Supreme Court of India","Court-appointed technical committee","NSO Group"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Was surveillance used beyond legally authorised security purposes?","prediction":"Independently authenticated infections would align with operator procurement, authorisations and audit logs showing non-security targeting or missing lawful approval.","alternative":"Foreign operators, lawful security investigations, unrelated malware or incorrect attribution could explain parts of the record.","falsifier":"Credible forensic re-analysis disproves infections, or independently supervised records establish a different operator and lawful scope for the specific cases.","records":"Redacted technical report; chain-of-custody forensic results; procurement and authorisation records released lawfully; independent oversight findings. Do not expose victims' device contents."}]},{"id":"inst-ed-bansal","title":"ED arrest safeguards: Pankaj Bansal judgment","sector":"Enforcement / judiciary","status":"documented","summary":"On 3 October 2023, the Supreme Court invalidated Pankaj and Basant Bansal's arrests and criticised ED's handling of the case.","finding":"The Court found the agency failed the required standards of fairness in this case and required written grounds of arrest to be supplied. It emphasised that a remand court must independently check PMLA Section 19 compliance; an unlawful arrest cannot be validated by routine remand.","limitation":"The decision concerns arrest legality and agency conduct in a particular case. It neither acquits every accused of underlying offences nor proves party-directed selective enforcement. A population study would need all comparable cases, including those against government supporters.","sources":["inst-sc-bansal-2023"],"entities":["Directorate of Enforcement","Supreme Court of India","Pankaj Bansal","Basant Bansal","Remand courts"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Do institutional incentives encourage custody before a defensible arrest record is complete?","prediction":"A representative cohort would show repeated missing written grounds, weak remand scrutiny and reversals concentrated in identifiable offices or periods.","alternative":"A small number of officers made errors during evolving procedural interpretation rather than a coordinated campaign.","falsifier":"A complete, independently coded cohort shows durable compliance and no disproportionate pattern after controlling for offence and case stage.","records":"Public arrest-challenge judgments; written-ground service receipts where lawfully disclosed; remand orders; agency compliance circulars; disciplinary outcomes; complete case denominators."}]},{"id":"inst-newsclick-arrest","title":"NewsClick: UAPA arrest and remand held invalid","sector":"Police / press freedom","status":"documented","summary":"The Supreme Court's 15 May 2024 judgment invalidated Prabir Purkayastha's arrest and remand because required grounds were not provided in time.","finding":"The Court distinguished generic reasons from personal grounds of arrest, rejected subsequent remand as a cure, and ordered release subject to the trial court's bond conditions. It noted later insertions in the remand order while explicitly allowing that they might have been made without harmful intent. It declined to decide the underlying allegations.","limitation":"This concerns FIR 224/2023 and procedural rights, not an acquittal or a finding of fabricated foreign influence. The separate 2020 EOW/PMLA proceedings and FEMA adjudication require separate records. Political direction cannot be inferred from agency hierarchy alone.","sources":["inst-sc-purkayastha-2024"],"entities":["Delhi Police Special Cell","Prabir Purkayastha","NewsClick","Supreme Court of India","Remand court"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Were procedural shortcuts used to obtain detention before effective legal challenge?","prediction":"Authenticated timestamps would show grounds and counsel access systematically following rather than preceding completed remand decisions in comparable cases.","alternative":"Hasty scheduling and recordkeeping failures explain the sequence without central coordination.","falsifier":"Independent timestamps establish timely meaningful disclosure and counsel access across the comparator cohort; no persistent post-judgment pattern remains.","records":"Arrest and service timestamps; counsel notification records; original remand record; compliance circulars; comparable UAPA arrest-challenge decisions, coded without partisan selection."}]},{"id":"inst-newsclick-predicate","title":"NewsClick: 2020 FIR and linked PMLA case quashed in 2026","sector":"Enforcement / foreign funding","status":"documented","summary":"The Delhi High Court's 29 May 2026 judgment quashed FIR 116/2020 and the linked ECIR/14/HIU/2020.","finding":"In paragraphs 124–132 the Court concluded that the allegations did not disclose the invoked offences; an agreement alone did not establish criminal conspiracy without an illegal objective or means. Without the predicate case, the connected PMLA proceedings could not survive. The judgment is distinct from the 2024 UAPA arrest ruling.","limitation":"LawChakra reported an ED appeal on 11 October 2026, citing Diary 63217/2026; its official docket and any stay were not inspected. Do not describe the litigation as finally closed or this ruling as disposing of UAPA or FEMA proceedings. Earlier party submissions in the judgment must not be mistaken for holdings.","sources":["inst-dhc-newsclick-2026","inst-newsclick-appeal-report"],"entities":["Delhi Police Economic Offences Wing","Directorate of Enforcement","Delhi High Court","PPK NewsClick Studio Private Limited","Prabir Purkayastha"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Was the initial financial investigation sustained without a legally sufficient predicate offence?","prediction":"Contemporaneous case-review records would show continued coercive steps despite known deficiencies in the offence elements and no new supporting material.","alternative":"Investigators genuinely but incorrectly interpreted financial rules or expected evidence that did not materialise.","falsifier":"Appellate review identifies a legally sustainable predicate supported by contemporaneous evidence, or records demonstrate prompt correction when deficiencies became clear.","records":"Certified High Court judgment; official appeal docket and orders; initial complaint and referral; dated legal-review notes lawfully disclosed; coercive-action chronology."}]},{"id":"inst-demolition-process","title":"Punitive demolition: Supreme Court's nationwide safeguards","sector":"Police / local administration / judiciary","status":"documented","summary":"On 13 November 2024, the Supreme Court set nationwide safeguards against arbitrary demolition of homes and other structures.","finding":"The Court rejected executive punishment through demolition and discussed selective targeting as capable of supporting a rebuttable inference of improper motive. It required notice, hearing, reasoned orders, time for challenge, and documented demolition, with exceptions for specified public-place encroachments and court-ordered demolition. It warned of personal restitution liability for violations.","limitation":"The judgment supplies legal standards; it does not establish that every demolition is unlawful or centrally directed. Land-use enforcement is chiefly a state and local function, and each case needs its own law, notices and evidence. Later compliance has not been comprehensively measured.","sources":["inst-sc-demolition-2024"],"entities":["Supreme Court of India","State governments","Municipal authorities","District magistrates","Police"],"hypotheses":[{"question":"Are building rules applied selectively as extra-judicial punishment?","prediction":"Properties associated with recently accused people face unusually fast demolition while nearby matched violations persist, with weak notice or appeal access.","alternative":"Pre-existing enforcement schedules, distinct land categories or court orders explain the timing and different treatment.","falsifier":"Pre-dated notices, uniform comparable enforcement and valid exception records account for the observed actions.","records":"Municipal notice and demolition registers; court orders; land classifications; dated maps and imagery; matched nearby enforcement records; hearing minutes; restitution and contempt outcomes."}]}]`),d=[{id:"inst-cag-pmjay-2023",title:"CAG Report 11 of 2023: Performance Audit of AB-PMJAY",url:"https://d7i5wg8xwe4hf.cloudfront.net/uploads/download_audit_report/2023/Report-No.-11-of-2023_PA-on-PMJAY_English-PDF-A-064d22bab2b83b5.38721048.pdf",publisher:"Comptroller and Auditor General of India",date:"2023",access:"full-text",supports:"Beneficiary validation (para 3.6.5, printed p.19); mortality claim flags (para 5.8.2.8, printed pp.53–54); agency replies. Official report linked from CAG report page.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-mohfw-pmjay-response",title:"Ministry response to media interpretations of PM-JAY audit",url:"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1949844&lang=2&reg=3",publisher:"Ministry of Health and Family Welfare / PIB",date:"2023-08-17",access:"full-text",supports:"Official explanations for delayed pre-authorisation, parent–child card use and non-mandatory mobile numbers. Agency response, not independent verification.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-cag-pmkvy-2025",title:"CAG Report 20 of 2025: Skill Development under PMKVY",url:"https://d7i5wg8xwe4hf.cloudfront.net/uploads/download_audit_report/2025/Report-No.-20-of-2025_PA-PMKVY_English-PDF-A-06943abec463479.68516873.pdf",publisher:"Comptroller and Auditor General of India",date:"2025-12-18",access:"full-text",supports:"First three phases, 2015–22; paras 3.8.3.1–3.8.3.2, printed pp.58–61, reused photographs, multiple-state inspection records, ministry explanations and 2025 changes.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-sc-pegasus-2021",title:"Manohar Lal Sharma v Union of India: technical committee order",url:"https://www.livelaw.in/pdf_upload/pegasus-order-supreme-court-403008.pdf",publisher:"Supreme Court of India (judgment reproduced by LiveLaw)",date:"2021-10-27",access:"full-text",supports:"Paras 45–55: inadequate limited affidavit, national-security scrutiny, independent technical inquiry. Does not decide operator attribution.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-sco-pegasus-2025",title:"Pegasus hearing: petitioners seek disclosure of sealed report",url:"https://www.scobserver.in/reports/pegasus-spyware-probe-day-8-petitioners-demand-revelation-of-sealed-cover-report/",publisher:"Supreme Court Observer",date:"2025-04-29",access:"full-text",supports:"Reported hearing; committee did not confirm Pegasus in five malware-positive devices among 29 examined; disclosure contested. Secondary report, not access to sealed technical report.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-sco-pegasus-status",title:"Pegasus Spyware Probe case overview",url:"https://www.scobserver.in/cases/manohar-lal-sharma-v-union-of-india-pegasus-spyware-probe-case-background/",publisher:"Supreme Court Observer",date:"2025-07-16",access:"full-text",supports:"Page last-updated July 2025 labels case pending; not a certified October 2026 docket check.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-sc-bansal-2023",title:"Pankaj Bansal v Union of India, 2023 INSC 866",url:"https://api.sci.gov.in/supremecourt/2023/30275/30275_2023_16_1501_47281_Judgement_03-Oct-2023.pdf",publisher:"Supreme Court of India",date:"2023-10-03",access:"full-text",supports:"Arrest invalidation; written grounds; duty of remand court; case-specific criticism of ED conduct, especially para20 and concluding directions.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-sc-purkayastha-2024",title:"Prabir Purkayastha v State (NCT of Delhi), 2024 INSC 414",url:"https://api.sci.gov.in/supremecourt/2023/42896/42896_2023_3_1503_53250_Judgement_15-May-2024.pdf",publisher:"Supreme Court of India",date:"2024-05-15",access:"full-text",supports:"Paras38–50: grounds of arrest, remand timing and invalidation; para39 allows non-malicious explanation for insertions; para44 reserves merits.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-dhc-newsclick-2026",title:"PPK NewsClick Studio v State of NCT of Delhi, W.P.(CRL.)1130/2021 and connected petitions",url:"https://delhihighcourt.nic.in/app/showFileJudgment/NBK29052026CRLW11302021_190914.pdf",publisher:"Delhi High Court",date:"2026-05-29",access:"full-text",supports:"Paras124–132: absence of disclosed offence, insufficient conspiracy allegation, quashing of FIR116/2020 and ECIR14/HIU/2020. Read holdings separately from submissions.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-newsclick-appeal-report",title:"ED challenges Delhi High Court NewsClick ruling",url:"https://lawchakra.in/supreme-court/newsclick-laundering-case/",publisher:"LawChakra",date:"2026-10-11",access:"full-text",supports:"Reports appeal Diary63217/2026, reportedly filed October10. Official appellate docket not inspected; no claim of appellate disposition.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"},{id:"inst-sc-demolition-2024",title:"In Re: Directions in the Matter of Demolition of Structures, 2024 INSC 866",url:"https://api.sci.gov.in/supremecourt/2022/12239/12239_2022_2_1501_57147_Judgement_13-Nov-2024.pdf",publisher:"Supreme Court of India",date:"2024-11-13",access:"full-text",supports:"Paras82–94: rebuttable selective-enforcement inference, prohibition of collective punishment, procedural safeguards, exceptions and official liability.",accessedAt:"2026-10-14"}],t={cases:o,sources:d},c=[...e.cases,...i.cases,...t.cases],l=[...e.sources,...i.sources,...t.sources],u="2026-10-14",p={documented:"Documented record",allegation:"Attributed allegation","open-question":"Open question"};export{p as I,c as a,u as b,l as c};
